          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.753,
            0.047,
            0.862,
            0.063
          ],
          "content": "Number Play"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.084,
            0.801,
            0.124
          ],
          "content": "？ Examine each of the following statements, and determine whether it is ‛Always true’, ‛Sometimes true’, ‛Never true’."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.818,
            0.072,
            0.904,
            0.125
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_9_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.132,
            0.824,
            0.154
          ],
          "content": "？ 6. If a number is divisible by both 9 and 4, it must be divisible by 36."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.161,
            0.825,
            0.184
          ],
          "content": "？ 7. If a number is divisible by both 6 and 4, it must be divisible by 24."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.186,
            0.849,
            0.262
          ],
          "content": "**In general, if A is divisible by k and A is also divisible by m, then A is divisible by the LCM of k and m.** This is because A is a multiple of k and also a multiple of m, so A’s prime factorisation should contain the prime factorisation of LCM (k, m)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.265,
            0.847,
            0.303
          ],
          "content": "？ 8. When you add an odd number to an even number we get a multiple of 6."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.308,
            0.628,
            0.402
          ],
          "content": "We know that multiples of 6 are all even numbers. The sum of an odd number and an even number will be an odd number. Therefore, this statement is never true. We can also explain this algebraically. Suppose,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.289,
            0.406,
            0.477,
            0.426
          ],
          "content": "( 2 n ) + ( 2 m + 1 ) = 6 j ,",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.43,
            0.614,
            0.469
          ],
          "content": "where 2n is an even number, 2m + 1 is an odd number, and 6j is a multiple of 6. Then"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.302,
            0.474,
            0.464,
            0.518
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}2n + 2m &= 6j - 1 \\\\2\\ (n + m) &= 6j - 1\\end{aligned}",
          "image_source": "images/page_9_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.631,
            0.294,
            0.886,
            0.497
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_9_image_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.521,
            0.847,
            0.56
          ],
          "content": "which means $2 ( n \\mathrm { ~ + ~ } m )$ which is an even number, should be equal to 6j – 1, which is an odd number. This is never true."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.575,
            0.346,
            0.595
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "What Remains?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.605,
            0.847,
            0.643
          ],
          "content": "？ Find a number that has a remainder of 3 when divided by 5. Write more such numbers."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.646,
            0.711,
            0.669
          ],
          "content": "Which algebraic expression(s) capture all such numbers?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.194,
            0.671,
            0.847,
            0.695
          ],
          "content": "(i) $3 k + 5$ (ii) $3 k - 5$ (iii) $\\frac { 3 k } { 5 }$ (iv) $5 k \\pm 3$ (v) $5 k - 2$ (vi) $5 k   -   3$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.697,
            0.848,
            0.791
          ],
          "content": "The numbers that leave a remainder of 0 when divided by 5 are the multiples of 5. But we want numbers that leave a remainder of 3 when divided by 5. These numbers are 3 more than multiples of 5. Multiples of 5 are of the form 5k. So, numbers that leave a remainder of 3 when divided by 5 are those of the form 5k + 3"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.231,
            0.804,
            0.387,
            0.907
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{array}{c}\\stackrel{\\stackrel{\\frown}{\\underset{\\stackrel{\\cdot}{\\underset{\\cdot}{\\underset{\\cdot}{\\underset{\\cdot}{\\underset{\\cdot}{\\underset{\\cdot}{\\underset{\\cdot}{\\underset{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\underset{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\underset{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\underset{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\underset{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\underset{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\underset{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\underset{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\underset{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\underset{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\underset{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\underset{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\underset{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\underset{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\underset{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\underset{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\underset{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\underset{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\underset{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\underset{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\underset{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\cdot}{\\c